    "7.12.45: third live follow-up on the conductor instance view, task a205eb7a again (an IN-PROGRESS task's rightmost/newest rail pill this time, not a done one). Owner: 'it is not clear what [behavior] is currently running... remember we have the logic to fill up the panel of the task while that workflow is active'. Investigated empirically before fixing: opened this exact task's instance mid-drive via Playwright and found TWO real defects. (1) A genuine data-freshness bug: fetchConductorRunFromTask (lib/useWorkflowDef.ts) built runtime.currentStep from the FRESH `history` its own request returns, but built data.conductorTask.workflowStep/gateState/status from the CALLER's `task` argument -- the rail's own last SSE push/10s poll, which can be a step or two behind a task advancing every 1-2 minutes. Caught live: the top badge read '...verify green state' while the canvas/testStep simultaneously reflected 'green gate', because the real task had moved on between the rail's last poll and this fetch. Fixed by reading title/status/workflow_step/gate_state off the SAME task-detail response's `task` object (already returned by scope=core, previously ignored) so both signals are always the SAME snapshot. (2) A genuine legibility gap: even once consistent, the ONLY 'currently running' signal was the small (~85px) canvas node's own teal border + a 10px 'RUN Xs' corner label + a 3px top fill bar -- nothing like the large, DOM-rendered, segmented SdlcProgress bar (step name, %, live clock, ETA, state label) TaskDetailPage/PlanView already use for exactly this ('the logic to fill up the panel... while active'). Fixed by reusing SdlcProgress itself (not reimplementing it) inside the top badge for a live (non-done) conductor instance: a new conductorLivePhase/conductorLiveActivity pair synthesizes phase/activity client-side from data already on hand (the current step's average_duration_seconds off the workflow catalog, runtime.startedAt off the run) rather than a second, slower phase_progress fetch (scope=full costs ~30s cold) -- the same tradeoff the canvas's own activeProgress already makes. activity.state reuses ACTIVITY_META's existing vocabulary (awaiting_gate/blocked/working), never a new label. Verified live (Playwright, real project=prism task bbfd1a19 parked at green_gate/awaiting_gate): the top badge and the SdlcProgress caption now agree ('green gate' on both), and the caption legibly reads 'GREEN GATE · 90.91% ● waiting 26:09:55 · awaiting review' with the full 10-segment bar (9 solid emerald done-segments + 1 highlighted current segment) rendered beneath the one-line summary -- a dramatic legibility improvement over the prior tiny canvas indicator. Confirmed conductorLivePhase is null (no behavior change) for validation's own live-run badge and for a finished conductor replay -- this is additive, not a rewrite. New tests in test_workflows_section_ui.py pin the freshness fix and the SdlcProgress reuse/guard. "
    "7.12.44: task a205eb7a (the fresh 'Remove Simulate flow button' drive) reached status=done/full_outcome_complete=True while genuinely UNSHIPPED, discovered by directly verifying its green_gate pass rather than trusting it -- its own history had NO 'ship' action row at all, and its origin/main log had no matching commit. Root cause: its real driver committed with the trailer `[task:a205eb7a-d46b-4d1c-a2a0-809a0c1e3ff0]` -- the FULL uuid -- instead of the documented 8-char short form `[task:a205eb7a]`. Three call sites all assumed the short form EXACTLY (an 8-char id then an immediate closing bracket): conductor_service._unshipped_gate_reason's `--grep '[task:<id8>]'`, api.tasks._is_shipped_on_main's identical needle, and api.tasks._compute_stranded's regex `\\[task:([^\\]\\s]{8})\\]`. Reproduced empirically against the REAL task workspace: the old exact-bracket pattern found ZERO local commits (fail-opening _unshipped_gate_reason -- a deliberate behavior for a task that never committed anything, wrongly triggered here for one that HAD). Fixed all three to match the trailer as a PREFIX (open bracket + 8-char short id, no closing bracket required), so a full-uuid trailer and the documented short form are recognized identically. New test_shipped_ness_tolerates_full_uuid_trailer.py (6 tests): reproduces the exact live grep-miss against a real git fixture, confirms _unshipped_gate_reason now correctly refuses (was silently passing), confirms it still clears once genuinely shipped, confirms _is_shipped_on_main both ways, and confirms _compute_stranded's regex captures the short id from a full-uuid trailer. "
    "7.12.43: two follow-up defects in 7.12.42's conductor instance replay, both owner-reported live on this exact instance and both empirically verified (Playwright, real project=prism data) rather than assumed. (1) 'the animation on that playback appears to be made up' -- conductorTimelineFromHistory (lib/useWorkflowDef.ts) only ever read action=='advance_task' rows and stamped every closed segment status:'passed', silently discarding action=='flow_report_failure' (a step attempt that failed and retried), action=='advance_refused' (a review step's own validation refusing to advance), and a gate_decide whose real outcome was a rejection/control-plane failure -- so a task that genuinely struggled replayed as one smooth, unbroken green climb. Confirmed live against task 8a06e121 (real history: advance_refused at review_previous_notes, flow_report_failure at write_failing_tests and implement_tasks) and 93d6c6f3 (3 real flow_report_failure rows at verify_green_state). Fixed: new conductorFailedStepFromDetails recognizes all three real setback shapes (a verifier=fail gate_decide only counts absent an override=True, per api/tasks.py _build_timeline's own documented ambiguity) and the timeline builder now closes a failed attempt with status:'failed' and reopens a fresh dwell at the SAME step, so a step tried 3 times produces 3 failed segments + the eventual passed one. WorkflowsPage.tsx's replay walk now stamps the CURRENTLY-ANIMATING event's own status into a new replayEventStatusRef (distinct from replayStoppedAt, which only ever named the run's FINAL event) and the rAF loop tints a mid-walk failed segment with the SAME tone:'failure'/label:'FAILED' a terminal failure already renders -- no new vocabulary invented. (2) 'This playback of the animation is not moving from step to step, its still just doing the random animations' -- investigated empirically (frame-by-frame Playwright label/screenshot traces) before touching anything, per instruction: sendTransition DOES fire once per real timeline event in real order with a genuine 1.5-5s visible dwell (confirmed via a 150-200ms-interval label trace showing start -> review_previous_notes -> draft_story -> story_gate -> verify_plan -> plan_gate... on a real done task) -- the stepping mechanism itself was never broken. What WAS broken: the page's own def+occupancy poll (WorkflowsPage.tsx, `useEffect(..., [project])`, POLL_MS=10s) unconditionally called graphRef.current.setDef(workflowForGraph(selected)) on every tick, reapplying the LIVE BOARD's real occupancy over whatever synthetic single-node occupancy the replay had just set -- confirmed live via GET /api/workflows?project=prism: conductor's real occupancy right now is {green_gate:25, plan_gate:13, story_gate:5, write_failing_tests:2, ...} while EVERY other workflow's (validation included) is always {}, which is exactly why Build and test's own replay (verified side-by-side, same close label-trace method) never showed this and conductor's did -- workflowGraph.ts's `step()` ambient motion (a SEPARATE, deliberate feature: bot-ownership wires pulse whenever their step has real live occupancy) then fired continuously off that reapplied real occupancy, reading as random motion unrelated to the deliberate replay. Fixed with a `viewingInstanceRef` (a ref, because the poll's closure only ever sees the `[project]`-time state and would otherwise read a stale `false` forever) set true in replayHistoricalRun and false in leaveHistoricalReplay/selectWorkflow; the poll now skips reapplying live def/occupancy while an instance overlay is open. Re-verified after the fix: label trace over the SAME real task now shows the identical clean step-by-step cadence Build and test has always had, and a mid-replay screenshot shows only the single active node highlighted with zero bleed-through from the live board's real, heavily-occupied nodes. A zoomed screenshot at the review_previous_notes retry additionally confirms fix (1) visually: the node shows a red border + FAILED badge during its failed sub-segment, then a normal teal/active border on its very next (passed) sub-segment. New/updated tests in test_workflows_section_ui.py pin conductorFailedStepFromDetails's three setback shapes, the timeline builder's close-and-reopen behavior, and the mid-walk failure tone/label reuse. "
    "7.12.42: the conductor rail's pill click never opened anything like Build and test's instance view -- owner, looking at both rails directly: '[the build/test bar] opens the INSTANCE of the workflow its describing, theres a whole animation and everything, please make sure the conductor view is using that same logic'. Confirmed by reading the source: validation's rail onClick called replayHistoricalRun(run), which opens the curtain overlay (WorkflowsPage.tsx:~1520) and animates the run's real timeline through the FSM canvas at 120x (beginHistoricalReplay); the conductor rail's onClick was simply navigate(`/tasks/${task.id}`) -- a plain redirect away from the page, no overlay, no animation, no reuse of any of that machinery. Fixed by REUSING the same replayHistoricalRun/beginHistoricalReplay pipeline for conductor instead of building a second one: new fetchConductorRunFromTask (lib/useWorkflowDef.ts) synthesizes a WorkflowRun off the task's OWN audit history (conductor_service.advance_task's `action=advance_task, details=from=X; to=Y` rows -- there is no separate WorkflowCore run for a conductor task, this history IS the instance record), then hands it to the same replayHistoricalRun the validation rail already calls. Conductor data is genuinely different from a scripted run's, so it is NOT forced into the tests/build fields (now optional on WorkflowRun): a new `data.conductorTask` carries title/status/workflow_step/gate_state/stranded, and a new conductorRunSummary/conductorRunTone render the top badge as e.g. 'Validation runs tests and builds · green gate · awaiting gate' instead of the build/test sentence. A task still in flight (not done) gets a `runtime` block instead of a closed timeline, so beginHistoricalReplay shows its live current-step progress (reusing the SAME activeProgress rendering a running scripted workflow's step already gets) rather than replaying an unfinished history; a done task gets the full timeline and replays start to finish exactly like a completed validation run, ending green/amber per its own stranded-ness. Also fixed two latent bugs the reuse exposed: the validation-only live-run poller (GET /api/workflows/runs/:id, a WorkflowCore-instance route) would have 404'd forever against a conductor task id -- guarded off for the conductor workflow; and selectedHistoryRun resolved off workflowRunHistory (validation-only, always empty for conductor) -- now resolves off workflowRun itself when the conductor workflow is selected. Verified live in a real browser (Playwright, vite dev server against the AOS-hosted dev backend, real prism-project tasks): clicked an in-flight task's pill (green_gate/awaiting gate) -- overlay opened, badge read 'Validation runs tests and builds · green gate · awaiting gate', green_gate node ring-highlighted, no timeline replay (live progress instead); clicked a done task's pill (10 real advance_task rows) -- overlay opened, badge read '... · shipped', frame emerald, and the canvas visibly animated review_previous_notes -> plan_gate -> ... at REPLAY 120x, same as validation. Confirmed Build and test's own rail is pixel-for-pixel unchanged (build/test badge, red frame, REPLAY 120x · TEST) -- no regression. New tests in test_workflows_section_ui.py pin the reuse (onClick calls openConductorInstance -> fetchConductorRunFromTask -> replayHistoricalRun, never navigate), the conductor-specific data shape, the live-vs-replay branch in beginHistoricalReplay, and the two latent-bug guards. "
    "7.12.41: 7.12.39's land-nesting fix shipped correct but mispositioned -- confirmed live via a teammate's Playwright screenshot: 'land' rendered as the 2nd item under CONDUCTOR (right after 'Build and test'), not as the LAST item the owner actually asked for ('a final ship step when it's all done'). Root cause: _conductor_behavior_workflows renders entries in the same order bot.json's own behaviorIds lists them, and 'land' was first in that list (predating this session, unrelated to the parent_id nesting logic 7.12.39 fixed). Moved 'land' to the END of .prism/behaviors/conductor/bot.json's behaviorIds -- now the true terminal item, after green-gate-status. New test_land_is_ordered_last_in_bot_json_so_it_renders_as_the_terminal_step pins the checked-in file directly (same discipline as the land.json shape test). Also: separately, while syncing this session's fixes into the shared AOS-hosted checkout, found and restored ~6 files a different, accidentally-launched-then-killed agent had staged a revert of (undoing 7.12.38's ship_worker fix and 7.12.39's land nesting) -- unrelated to this fix's content, noted here only because it's why the live daemon briefly served stale 7.12.36 despite dev already being at 7.12.39. "
    "7.12.40: owner report -- manually positioned nodes on the /workflows canvas reset. Investigated first: this was NOT a missing-persistence bug (task f506ece4, 2026-08-18, already writes drag-end node positions to prism.workflows.positions.<project> in localStorage, and rehydrates them on mount before first paint -- test_positions_are_remembered_per_project already pinned this). The actual root cause, confirmed empirically with a Playwright drag/reload/re-navigate cycle: WorkflowsPage's selectWorkflow() -- fired by every directory click, drilling into a linked workflow (e.g. verify_green_state -> Build and test), and the breadcrumb back button -- calls graphRef.current.clearOverrides() (wiping node positions AND wire ports/waypoints) and never rehydrates from localStorage afterward. A plain reload looked fine because the initial-mount effect hydrates correctly, but the FIRST in-app navigation after a drag silently dropped the in-memory layout, and the very next drag's persist() call then overwrote localStorage with that emptied map, permanently destroying the saved layout. Fixed by making selectWorkflow rehydrate from localStorage: node positions BEFORE setDef (same ordering as the initial-mount effect, so nodes never visibly snap from default to saved), wire ports/waypoints AFTER setDef (they validate against the freshly built wire list, which only exists once setDef has run). Verified in a real browser (vite dev server against the running AOS dev backend): dragged implement_tasks to a new position, confirmed it survives a hard reload (pre-existing behavior), then navigated to Build and test and back to Conductor -- before the fix this would have reset the node to its auto-layout default; after the fix it stayed exactly where it was dragged, while every untouched node kept its normal auto-layout row position. New test_switching_workflows_rehydrates_the_owners_saved_layout in test_workflows_section_ui.py pins the ordering (clear -> rehydrate positions -> setDef -> rehydrate ports/waypoints) by source inspection, matching this suite's no-JS-test-runner convention. "
    "7.12.39: 7.12.38 made shipping actually RUN automatically (ship_worker's machine-track fix); this closes the visible half of the same ask -- owner, looking at the Workflows page sidebar directly: 'make the conductor's workflow have a final ship step when it's all done', because GREEN GATE EVIDENCE STATUS was the last item under CONDUCTOR and 'Land a task branch' rendered as a disconnected top-level sibling. Root cause: api/workflows.py's _CONDUCTOR_LINKED_BEHAVIOR_IDS only nests a behavior under conductor when a REAL state->behavior relationship exists (the codebase's own stated principle) -- and a prior session had explicitly left this as an open owner question ('does green_gate approval trigger land, or stay a manual action?'). Answering it: yes, automatically, on both tracks (human-approved ship-on-approve and the machine-adjudicated track built in 7.12.38) -- so 'land' now belongs in _CONDUCTOR_LINKED_BEHAVIOR_IDS, nesting via that set directly (green_gate is the FSM's structurally-terminal step -- 14+ call sites in conductor_service.py treat it as literally last -- so inserting a real new WORKFLOW_STEPS entry after it was audited and deliberately NOT attempted here; that's separately-scoped, higher-risk work). Also completed land.json itself, which only defined push+open-pr (half of ship_worker.py's real pipeline) -- added wait-for-ci (`gh pr checks \${branch} --watch`) and merge (`gh pr merge \${branch} --squash --delete-branch`) steps, each self-contained via \${branch}/\${taskId} context vars rather than depending on unconfirmed inter-step output-passing. NOT live-verified against the AosWorkflows engine (no confirmed reachable instance from this worktree) -- verified via 3 new/updated Python-side tests instead: test_land_is_the_conductors_visible_final_ship_step (land nests, ci-local-dev the negative control stays unparented), test_land_json_completes_the_real_ship_pipeline_shape (pins the checked-in JSON's 4-step shape), and 4 existing tests updated (they'd deliberately pinned land as UNparented -- the correct behavior before this decision, now superseded). 271 workflow/conductor tests green. "
    "7.12.38: the conductor's SDLC was missing its final step -- ship + validate -- for the ENTIRE machine-adjudicated (proof_type=test) track, which is most tasks. ship_worker's _awaiting_ship only ever found a task carrying a RECORDED HUMAN 'ship=queued' approval (the demo/review, PRISM_SHIP_ON_APPROVE track, task 5b6aefc1) -- a proof_type=test task never records that row, since no human approval is required for it (the adjudicator seat, task eaafdf75, owns its own green). So a fully-autonomous task could clear every OTHER green_gate tooth (fresh receipt, reachability, candidate-controls-judge, screen-claim) and then park FOREVER on _unshipped_gate_reason alone, because nothing else ever pushes/PRs/merges its branch. Owner: 'missing a final step in our conductor flow to ship and validate ... shipping software is pydantic' -- correctly rejecting an earlier draft of this report that conflated 'shipped' (merged to main, plain deterministic git+gh) with 'this session's own dev daemon has restarted to serve new code' (an unrelated, personal PRISM-on-PRISM dogfooding ritual with zero relationship to the conductor's own definition of shipped). Fixed: ship_task() takes a new on_landed(task_svc, cond, task_id) hook (default _replay_owner_approval, unchanged); new _awaiting_ship_machine() finds proof_type-non-demo/review tasks parked on shipped-ness; sweep_once() ships them through the SAME deterministic push->pr create->pr checks->pr merge->fetch pipeline test_ship_worker.py already proves, then calls the new _adjudicate_after_ship() -- the machine-track twin of _replay_owner_approval -- which re-runs adjudicate_green_gate so the SAME pre-flight teeth decide, now that shipped-ness is genuinely cleared. New test_ship_worker_machine_track.py (6 tests): eligibility (demo/review excluded, unshipped test-proof found, already-shipped skipped), ship_task's on_landed wiring proven against a REAL merge into a real bare origin, sweep_once wiring, and the load-bearing end-to-end case -- a real http-probe oracle task walked to a pending green_gate, refused pre-ship, shipped via sweep_once against a real git+FakeGh pipeline, and landing at gate_state=passed/status=done."
    "7.12.37: 7.12.34's implement_tasks commit instruction was prose, not a guarantee -- nothing stopped a driver from ignoring it. Built the mechanical tooth task 4f74dafc actually needs: ConductorService._uncommitted_changes_refusal, a new green_gate check that runs `git status --porcelain` (scoped to task.allowed_files when set) against the task's own workspace and REFUSES a fresh, passing EvidenceReceipt when the workspace still has uncommitted changes -- closing the exact gap where osp.current_tree_sha hashes the committed HEAD, tests run clean against whatever is actually on disk (committed or not), and a receipt reads as 'fresh' for an implementation that was never reachable from any commit. Wired into _oracle_receipt_refusal right after a fresh receipt is found, so every green_gate call site (gate_decide, adjudicate_green_gate, mint) inherits it for free. Per the owner's standing rule, this is a separate deterministic CHECK, not more inference-step prose. New test_green_gate_requires_a_real_commit.py (7 tests): the tooth in isolation (clean/dirty/allowed_files-scoped/no-workspace), plus the load-bearing integration case reproducing 4f74dafc's exact shape (a fresh+passing receipt on a dirty tree is now refused; a clean tree still passes unchanged). "
    "7.12.35: the conductor rail (7.12.25) never showed a DONE task at all -- useConductorState's managed_tasks() deliberately excludes status=='done' (the same doctrine that drops a finished task off the /conductor board), so a task that completed its entire drive left no trace on the rail: no green, nothing, indistinguishable from never having run. Owner caught this directly (task 4f74dafc, real completed drive, zero pills for it) and asked for the missing case: done should render SOLID GREEN, never the pulsing gate/working tones. Added a small poll (10s, same cadence this page already uses) of /api/tasks filtered to status=='done' + a real conductor workflow_step, merged into the rail alongside the live-managed tasks, sorted together by updated_at so the timeline reads as one history. conductorPillTone now checks status=='done' FIRST -> solid emerald, no animate-pulse. "
    "7.12.34: task 4f74dafc drove all the way to green_gate/status=done with its ACTUAL implementation sitting as an uncommitted working-tree diff in the task's own workspace -- never committed, never merged, never shippable. write_failing_tests correctly commits its tests-only [task:<id>] commit (CLAUDE.md documents that convention clearly), but implement_tasks's old instruction ('Smallest change that turns the failing tests green.') said nothing about committing, ROLE_RULES['dev'] has no commit rule either, and CLAUDE.md's only mention of an implementation commit is a passing 'before any implementation commit' clause inside the RED lesson, never its own directive. verify_green_state's tests pass fine against an uncommitted worktree, so nothing downstream catches this before green_gate. Fixed the same way as the three prior fixes: conductor_flow.py's implement_tasks _GUIDE entry now explicitly says to commit with a [task:<task_id>] trailer before reporting, and states the uncommitted-diff-is-not-shippable consequence. New test_implement_tasks_commit_instruction.py (4 tests). NOTE: this is an instruction fix, not a mechanical safeguard -- verify_green_state still doesn't CHECK for a real commit, so a future drive could still skip it; flagged, not built, since it's a bigger change than this session's scope. "
    "7.12.33: 7.12.32's final-message caveat SURVIVED into another live failure -- task 7477be2d passed story_gate, verify_plan wrote a genuinely compliant plan+diagram via its own tool calls, then its OWN final wrap-up ('What I did: ... Reported the full plan... which overwrote plan_doc. Next: this needs the owner to approve.') clobbered it again, despite the caveat. Root cause: it was appended to the END of a long, doctrine-laden prompt, where an LLM's habitual \"here's a summary of what I did\" close easily overrides one buried sentence -- and coincidentally, the summary's own prose ('AC-1..AC-5') was enough to fool plan_coverage's mechanical AC-id diff into passing, so the corruption wasn't even caught by the rubric this time. Fixed by extracting the caveat into a module-level _FINAL_MESSAGE_CAVEAT constant, PREPENDING it (primacy) instead of appending, and rewriting it as a hard rule with a concrete WRONG/RIGHT example instead of a soft caveat. All 4 step-contract test files updated (_base() now strips the new prefix, not just the doctrine suffix) -- 48 tests green. "
    "7.12.32: a THIRD distinct bug in the same live drive, different class from 7.12.30/31 (those were content-format issues; this is a capture-mechanism issue). task_runner's claude_cli.invoke() runs a driving session to max_turns, and task_runner._route_proof writes whatever result.final_text() returns as the step's official report, UNCONDITIONALLY -- so a driver that writes a fully compliant plan_doc via its own tool calls mid-session, then keeps reasoning and ends on a conversational sign-off ('Want me to approve plan_gate on your behalf...?'), has that sign-off silently OVERWRITE its own correct earlier content. Observed live on task 88d79ca9, AFTER 7.12.31's plan_coverage fix was already working -- passed story_gate, verify_plan wrote a genuinely compliant plan+diagram, then its own final message clobbered it. conductor_flow.py's _job() now appends one universal caveat to every AGENT step's instructions (never gates, which are never driven by claude_cli at all): the driver's LAST message IS the official report, verbatim, never a status update or a question. New test_step_final_message_caveat.py (11 tests) pins this present on all 6 agent steps and absent on all 4 gates, exercising _job() directly against a minimal fake task rather than a real multi-step drive. "
    "7.12.31: same class of bug as 7.12.30, one step later. _verify_rubric_gate (conductor_service.py:3049-3051) deliberately reads plan_coverage's story_md AND plan_doc evidence off the SAME task.plan_doc field, because verify_plan's own report REPLACES plan_doc (task_runner._route_proof routes both draft_story and verify_plan to the same field) -- but the old verify_plan instruction ('Verify the plan covers the story (plan_coverage).') never said that, so a driver wrote a short standalone coverage note that wiped the story's AC-<n> lines from the only field the rubric can read them from. Observed live on task 90ad4c39: passed story_gate cleanly (7.12.30's fix working), then failed plan_gate with 'story carries no AC-<n> ids to diff coverage against' for exactly this reason. conductor_flow.py's verify_plan _GUIDE entry now states the report REPLACES plan_doc and every AC-<n> line must survive VERBATIM into it. New test_verify_plan_step_states_its_contract.py (8 tests) proves this against the REAL scorer via the REAL evidence shape (story_md and plan_doc as the SAME field, not the two-separate-fields shape other rubric tests use -- that shape is exactly what hid this bug), and pins the live failure still reproducing and staying refused. "
    "7.12.30: draft_story's driver instruction was underspecified relative to the story_complete rubric that scores it -- observed live, twice, on two separately-driven real tasks (785022b3, then its retry b9772333), both refused at story_gate on the first pass ('no acceptance criteria with AC-<n> ids found', then 'missing required section(s)') despite a 'Summary/Requirements/Acceptance Criteria with AC ids + oracles' instruction that named the right CONCEPTS but never stated the MECHANICAL format the parser checks: exact heading text, the literal 'AC-<n>' token, the literal 'oracle:' marker. conductor_flow.py's draft_story _GUIDE entry now states the contract precisely (heading names, a concrete AC-1/AC-2 example, where 'oracle:' must appear, and the refusal consequence), mirroring the same discipline review_previous_notes' premise_grounded instruction already uses. New test_draft_story_step_states_its_contract.py (10 tests) proves this end-to-end against the REAL scorer, not just wording: a story written exactly to the new instruction's guidance actually passes score_story_complete, and the two live failure modes still reproduce and stay refused (the fix is a better instruction, never a loosened rubric). "
    "7.12.29: task_runner is now event-driven instead of pure fixed-interval polling. Observed live on task 785022b3: with ZERO other eligible tasks competing for the slot, a single uncontested task still sat idle ~16 real minutes for its retry, because _loop's only wake mechanism was time.sleep(interval_s) -- task_service.update() was already publishing a real 'task.changed' event (for /sse/tasks) the instant a task became eligible, but task_runner had no way to hear it. Added task_runner.wake() (a threading.Event the loop waits on with the interval as a fallback timeout, not the primary trigger) and call it from the SAME task.changed publish path in task_service.py, wrapped in the same never-fail-the-write try/except. PRISM_TASK_RUNNER_INTERVAL now only bounds worst-case latency for changes this module doesn't observe (direct DB writes, crash recovery) -- a task becoming eligible is swept within the same tick cycle, not up to 900s later. Two new tests: the wake actually cuts a 999s wait short (not just accepted without observably doing anything), and task_svc.update() genuinely calls wake(). "
    "7.12.28: fixed conductor's bottom rail growing from the WRONG edge -- 7.12.25 shipped it filling from the right (conductorRailTasks[index - (RUN_RAIL_PILLS - conductorRailTasks.length)]) while validation's own rail (visibleRunHistory[index], no offset) fills from the left, so conductor's real pills sat packed against the right edge with a long dead run of disabled slots on the left -- the exact opposite of validation's own convention, caught by the owner directly off a screenshot after I claimed this was already verified. Fixed by indexing conductorRailTasks[index] directly, matching validation exactly. Should have been caught before reporting done; visual verification means actually checking the fill DIRECTION, not just that some pills render colored. "
    "7.12.27: follow-up to 7.12.26's bot swimlane left-align fix -- \"architect\" is a real defined bot on the conductor FSM that owns ZERO of the 10 conductor steps (no step carries persona=architect), so 7.12.26's fallback (the old even-spread formula) landed its label directly on top of \"dev\"/Builder's now-correctly-aligned label. Fixed by not rendering a bot node at all when it owns no step -- the wire loop already draws it zero wires, so a fallback position just repeats the original unanchored-label defect for a different bot. "
    "7.12.26: fixed the conductor canvas's bot swimlane labels (Steward/Verifier/Builder/Architect) floating disconnected from the columns they own. live/workflowGraph.ts's bot placement used an even count-based spread across the full chain width, independent of which steps a bot actually owns -- a bot owning only later steps floated over empty canvas with no visible column beneath it (Architect, in particular, had no wire landing under its label). Now each bot's label left-aligns to the SAME x its leftmost owned step gets from `place()`, not a separate guess. One shared fix in workflowGraph.ts -- both WorkflowsPage and LivePage render through this same geometry function, so no duplicate implementation to keep in sync. "
    "7.12.25: WorkflowsPage's bottom rail now fills in for the conductor workflow too, not just validation. Conductor drives real tasks through Python (advance_task/gate_decide), never through AosWorkflows, so there is no WorkflowCore run to poll -- the rail is sourced from useConductorState (the same live, SSE-pushed GET /api/conductor/state feed LiveBar.tsx already reads), filtered to tasks whose workflow_step is a real conductor step. Clicking a pill navigates to /tasks/{id}, mirroring LiveBar's existing 'whole row is one Link' pattern -- no replay, no invented WorkflowCore-instance tracking. Refactored to ONE shared pill-rail render path: both validation and conductor now produce the same {tone,title,onClick} RailPill shape and render through a single component, instead of two parallel JSX blocks. "
    "7.12.23: red_gate gets real governance visibility WITHOUT any FSM restructuring -- corrected course after a failed attempt (this session) to delete story_gate from WORKFLOW_STEPS and inline-check it broke 121 tests, because check_at_step has no override path the deleted gate had. The actual fix: the gate itself stays a real, untouched workflow_step (still decided exactly as before, conductor_service.py/conductor_flow.py/gate_adjudicator.py/models/workflow.py all byte-identical to 7.12.22) -- what was missing was VISIBILITY into what's happening at it. New POST /api/workflows/steps/red-gate-status is read-only, reuses the exact pure-read functions the real adjudicator consults (oracle_spec.fresh_red_receipt/latest_receipt, ConductorService._red_step_sha), never calls adjudicate_test_red_gate or any of its writes. red_gate now links to this behavior the same way story_gate/plan_gate already do. Verified: 829 passed, 0 failed on the full conductor/gate/workflow/adjudicator test slice (vs. 121 failures on the reverted delete-the-gate attempt). "
    "7.12.22: write_failing_tests and implement_tasks are the seventh and eighth conductor states with real linked behaviors -- Observe+Reason only (draft test code / draft implementation notes as TEXT, never written to disk), rubric=\"\" so Validate is explicitly reported as skipped rather than faked, since red_with_trace/green need real test execution with no YAML rubric to score against. 8 of 10 conductor states now real; red_gate/green_gate remain out of scope -- confirmed no pure function exists to extract, real writes are baked into the same call path as the read. "
    "7.12.21: Fixed a real bug caught by a live verify-plan-loop run: _score_rubric's plan_coverage branch read a \"story_md\" field no reason-loop schema ever produces (verify-plan-loop's schema only has plan_doc/plan_diagram), so AC-coverage always failed on an empty story. Fixed to mirror plan-gate-check/conductor_service.py exactly: the SAME plan_doc value is diffed against itself for AC ids. "
    "7.12.20: review_previous_notes and verify_plan are the fifth and sixth conductor states with real linked behaviors -- both PURE DATA (review-previous-notes-loop.json, verify-plan-loop.json), zero new Python code, reusing the generic /steps/reason-loop endpoint and the existing score_premise_grounded/score_plan_coverage scorers. 6 of 10 conductor states now real; write_failing_tests (red_with_trace) and implement_tasks (green) remain -- confirmed no YAML rubric exists for either, they need real test execution, not text scoring, so they don't fit this same cheap pattern. "
    "7.12.19: GENERIC Observe->Reason->Validate loop -- POST /api/workflows/steps/reason-loop, data-driven (persona/prompt/json_schema/rubric), so every conductor authoring state reuses ONE endpoint via its own behavior JSON instead of a bespoke Python function per state (owner correction: only Act is genuinely custom code, the rest is implicit). draft_story is the first consumer (draft-story-loop.json), the fourth conductor state with a real linked behavior. Also added Phase 1 of the migration plan to inference/claude_cli.py: json_schema param on invoke()/_build_cmd(), a structured_output field on ClaudeCliResult, verified against a REAL live claude -p call (haiku, $0.053, needs max_turns>=3 -- schema-constrained output costs a retry turn). Act deliberately NOT implemented -- inert proof only, per owner's explicit choice. Also fixed a real, never-before-exercised bug: story-gate-check.json/plan-gate-check.json's http-callback URLs never included ?project=, and AosWorkflows never auto-injected it -- would have silently 422'd if actually invoked through the real pipeline (only ever curled directly before). AosWorkflows now auto-injects ${project}/${repoPath}/${botId}/${behaviorId}/${prismBackendUrl} into every step's template, caller Vars still override. "
    "7.12.18: plan_gate is the THIRD conductor state with a real linked behavior. New POST /api/workflows/steps/plan-gate-check wraps the existing, unmodified arc_governance.score_plan_coverage rubric scorer -- read-only, mirrors conductor_service.py's _verify_rubric_gate evidence construction exactly (story_md gets the same value as plan_doc, principles sourced from get_project(project).memory_svc). Does not touch plan_gate's real autoclear logic. 3 of 10 conductor states now have real behaviors (verify_green_state, story_gate, plan_gate); the remaining 7 need either Phase 1 (schema-constrained claude -p, for the 5 authoring steps) or larger red_gate/green_gate extraction work -- not one-off cheap wraps. "
    "7.12.17: story_gate is the SECOND conductor state with a real linked behavior (verify_green_state->validation was the first). New POST /api/workflows/steps/story-gate-check wraps the existing, unmodified arc_governance.score_story_complete rubric scorer behind a typed contract -- read-only, does not touch conductor_flow.py's real story_gate autoclear logic. New AosWorkflows behavior 'story-gate-check' (http-callback kind), registered under the conductor bot. Implemented via a cheap Haiku subagent whose worktree had branched from a stale point missing this session's work -- verified its logic against the real, unchanged arc_governance.py, then manually ported only the new pieces onto the current dev branch rather than trusting its full diff. "
    "7.12.16: Un-nested land/ci-local-dev from conductor -- the conductor IS its 10-state FSM (WORKFLOW_STEPS), and progressive disclosure under it should only show what an actual state calls (verify_green_state -> validation, via linked_workflow_id, which is why 'Build and test' correctly stays nested). No conductor state calls land or ci-local-dev today, so nesting them there claimed a link that doesn't exist. They render as top-level entries now; owner decision, not mine, whether/which conductor state should eventually link to them. "
    "7.12.15: Reduced directory row left padding from px-5 (20px) to pl-2 (8px) on both the parent row's disclosure chevron and the child rows' drag handle, per owner ask for icons within 10px of the panel edge. Measured live with Playwright: icon left edge now sits ~10px from the panel's content edge. "
    "7.12.14: Actually fixed the icon alignment this time -- pl-11 was force-indenting child rows an extra ~24px past the parent row's own icon column instead of sharing it. Child rows now use the same px-5/w-4/shrink-0/text-center icon column as the parent's disclosure chevron, so drag handles sit in one consistent left-aligned column across parent and child rows, not a deeper nested indent. Verified with Playwright against the live page, not just reasoned about from source. "
    "7.12.13: Fixed the directory's nested-child row alignment -- items-center was vertically centering the drag handle and count against a wrapped 2-line label (e.g. 'Land a task branch'), making it float away from the top-left instead of sitting flush with the first line. Switched to items-start with a 1px top nudge on the icon/count so they line up with the text's cap-height regardless of how many lines the label wraps to. "
    "7.12.12: Drag-to-reorder for a bot's nested directory children (drag land/build-and-test/ci-local-dev above or below each other under CONDUCTOR). Order is a client-side preference (localStorage, per project, keyed by parent id) -- same tier as node positions and wire edits, never sent to the service. Native HTML5 drag/drop on the directory list, drop position determined by cursor vs. target midpoint. "
    "7.12.11: Nested 'Build and test' (validation) under conductor too -- it's the conductor's own capability (what its verify_green_state step links to), same category as land/ci-local-dev; it just predates the Bot/Behavior registry and is sourced differently, which is not a reason to render it as a flat sibling. "
    "7.12.10: Replaced the single synthetic 'conductor-behaviors' wrapper node with one real catalog entry PER behavior (land, ci-local-dev), each nested under conductor and disclosing its OWN actual steps (land: push/open-pr; ci-local-dev: test/version-bump/restart-dev) via a new AosWorkflows GET /workflows/bots/{botId}/behaviors/{behaviorId} read endpoint -- not fake placeholder nodes standing in for the whole behavior. execution stays 'connected' (not 'scripted') since /{workflow_id}/runs isn't wired to dispatch to AosWorkflows' bot/behavior endpoint yet -- known follow-up, not silently implied. "
    "7.12.9: Directory now shows real progressive disclosure -- a catalog entry can carry parent_id (conductor-behaviors nests under conductor) instead of every entry rendering as a flat sibling. WorkflowsPage.tsx directory nav groups by parent_id with a per-entry expand/collapse chevron (conductor starts expanded); WorkflowStepType gained \"behavior\"; web_dist rebuilt and verified the new chunk actually contains the nesting code. "
    "7.12.8: Fixed the Bot/Behavior ontology -- was a flat Bot->Behavior shape, corrected to Bot [1] uses FSM [1..*], FSM [1] has Behavior [0..*] (AosWorkflows Program.cs BotDefinition/FsmBehaviors/BehaviorDefinition, and this service's _conductor_behaviors_workflow reads the nested fsms[] shape). Behavior run URLs now name their owning fsm explicitly: POST /workflows/bots/{project}/{botId}/{fsmId}/{behaviorId}. "
    "7.12.7: GET /api/workflows now folds in the conductor bot's declared AosWorkflows behaviors (proxying GET /workflows/bots/conductor) as a new 'conductor-behaviors' catalog entry, alongside the existing conductor/validation entries -- so bot behaviors (land, ci-local-dev) show up on the /workflows page instead of only being reachable via raw curl to AosWorkflows. Fails soft (returns nothing extra) if AosWorkflows is unreachable or the bot isn't registered yet. See [[project_prism_workflow_engine_migration]]. "
    "7.12.6: PHASE 0 OF THE BOT/BEHAVIOR FSM MIGRATION. New POST /api/workflows/steps/context-enrich route wraps the existing ContextBuilder.build() behind a typed contract an AosWorkflows step can call directly -- read-only, no side effects. Verified end-to-end: a real AosWorkflows AgentStep call produced one connected OTel trace spanning both services and 109KB of genuine enrichment data. See [[project_prism_workflow_engine_migration]]. "
    "7.12.5: NO MORE GRATUITOUS WIRE ZIGZAGS ON /live AND /workflows. channelElbow tried its lane-offset candidate FIRST and kept it whenever it happened not to cross an obstacle -- true for nearly every wire, obstacle or not -- so with /live simplify off (an earlier fix for a different flattening bug) an unneeded lane jog rendered as a permanent staircase instead of a clean line. Now tries the centered path first, falling back to the lane offset only when something real is in the way. Both boards share this router (wireEditing.ts), so one fix covers both. "
    "7.12.4: NO MORE DEAD-END APPROVE FOR MACHINE-GRADED GATES. gate/readiness only asked the shipped-ness question inside the demo/review branch -- a proof_type=test task with a passing oracle but an unshipped commit read receipt_ok:true and a live clickable Approve that then dead-ended, because gate_decide checks shipped-ness unconditionally and readiness never did. Same tooth, now asked in both places; flat refusal, no ship-on-approve escape for machine-graded gates (task 8a06e121). "
    "7.12.3: RED_GATE VERIFIER NO LONGER CRASHES ON A TOOL TIMEOUT. _run_tool passed text=True to subprocess.run, but TimeoutExpired.stdout/stderr carry raw bytes regardless -- the text-decode step only runs on the success path. That bytes value flowed into a Claim.evidence dict and blew up json.dumps() at persist time (task 8a06e121). Decode at the source in the except clause instead. "
    "7.12.2: GIT REF-COUNTING COMPAT WITH GIT 2.34. Stranded-branch "
    "ahead/behind counts now fall back to a per-branch rev-list path when "
    "the single-pass %(ahead-behind) for-each-ref format (Git 2.41+) isn't "
    "available, instead of failing outright on older Git. "
    "7.12.1: LIVE CLAUDE SUBSCRIPTION USAGE IN SETTINGS. The Claude connector "
    "card now shows real usage via a new /api/claude-auth/usage endpoint (api.anthropic.com/api/oauth/usage) as a progress bar with reset time, "
    "polling every 60s. Usage polling mounts only once the card's detail "
    "panel is opened, not on every page load. "
    "7.12.0: VALIDATION RUNS OWN THEIR REPAIR SOURCE. PRISM captures an "
    "immutable Git snapshot before starting scripted validation; AOS persists "
    "that provenance with the run, and workflow_fix_request reconstructs the "
    "conductor workspace from the same commit instead of ambient HEAD. The "
    "caller now receives a typed conductor.step_validation result while raw "
    "process output remains supporting evidence. Legacy runs without a "
    "reconstructable snapshot fail closed. "
    "7.11.39: ASPIRE-MANAGED PRISM WEB HOT RELOAD. The AppHost now owns a "
    "continuous Vite production-bundle watcher, /api/version exposes the "
    "emitted index fingerprint, and dev-mode browser tabs reload when that "
    "fingerprint changes. Workflow navigation also uses the shared sidebar "
    "menu grammar on a full-bleed graph surface. "
    "7.11.33: ONE SHARED SSE STREAM PER BROWSER [task b835f639]. The Live tab "
    "could hang for tens of seconds on a fresh click while the server answered "
    "in single-digit ms: PRISM was starving its own browser connection pool. "
    "Global chrome opened THREE long-lived streams on every page (version.ts's "
    "/sse/live watchdog, useConductorState's /sse/sessions, and a SECOND "
    "/sse/sessions LiveBar opened for the identical URL), plus /sse/work on "
    "Live — so two tabs hit the ~6-per-origin HTTP/1.1 cap and a third tab "
    "could not fetch even its own HTML (measured: >60s to load /sessions with "
    "three tabs open, 62ms with them closed, while curl served that same "
    "document in 2.9ms). lib/sharedStream.ts now owns every EventSource: "
    "ref-counted per URL so subscribers share one socket, and a Web Locks "
    "leader tab holds the connections and rebroadcasts frames over a "
    "BroadcastChannel so follower tabs hold ZERO and stay live. LiveBar's "
    "duplicate subscription is deleted outright. No server change. "
    "7.11.29: A PER-PROJECT WORKFLOWS SECTION ON THE CANVAS [task f506ece4]. "
    "/workflows draws the conductor FSM as a wired chain with the four bots "
    "(Steward/Verifier/Builder/Architect) wired to the steps they own, a "
    "count badge where live tasks are standing, and ambient packets on the "
