    "\n7.13.117: \"bot\" is no longer a synonym of Agent in model-lexicon.ttl (owner 2026-08-27, mx-0e5a88: a Bot is a tier-1 deterministic workflow; agentic behaviours sit under it). The aligner had rewritten the ticket that describes the fix into \"Agent is its own term, not a synonym of Agent\". Guard: test_bot_is_never_a_synonym. The Bot / FSM / State / Transition / Behavior classes come with task 8bcd4cb3 under epic 12029f92."
    "\n7.13.119: StepRail per-step bar track is HALF the row (w-[50%], min 220px) instead of a 220px stub at the right edge - owner 2026-08-27: \"this bar is not taking up the length of the area STILL ... it should be like 40%-60%\"; 7.13.114-116 had only changed the fill math. test_step_meta_track_width_is_fixed_not_data_dependent now accepts a fixed row share (w-[40-60%]) as label-independent."
    "\n7.13.120: 7.13.119's w-[50%] never actually rendered wider (owner, 3rd report: \"you keep saying its working, but we can see its not\"). Root cause: for a real agent-work step, StepMeta rendered NESTED inside an extra ml-auto/min-w-0 wrapper div that has no width of its own (shrinks to fit content) -- a CSS percentage width on a descendant of an auto-sized flex item resolves to 0 per spec, so it silently fell back to StepMeta's own min-w-[220px] floor every time, identical to the pre-7.13.119 pixel width despite the class correctly saying 50%. Gate rows never had this extra wrapper and were unaffected, which is why testing missed it twice. Fixed by rendering StepMeta as a DIRECT child of the row (which has a real, definite width) for the non-current-step case -- only the current step's status badges keep the inner wrapper now. New regression guard: test_step_meta_is_a_direct_row_child_for_agent_steps."
    "\n7.13.121: even at a correctly-resolving w-[50%], a large gap of unused dark space remained between the step label and where the bar started, because the bar was capped short of the real available room and right-aligned into a small slot (owner, 4th report: \"why is there STILL so much dark space, are you not using the remote browser to see all of the dead space on each of those lines?\"). Fixed by making StepMeta's outer wrapper flex-1 so it GROWS to fill every pixel left over after the label -- no fixed width, no cap, no gap. A short label now yields a wider bar than a long label; that is correct, not a regression. Superseded test_step_meta_track_width_is_fixed_not_data_dependent with test_step_meta_track_fills_remaining_row_space_with_no_gap."
    "\n7.13.122: the owner's plan stop is the design-packet approval (task c016667f) on a ROOT task; a CHILD clears plan_gate on the rubric alone (owner 2026-08-27). 7.13.113 (task 3c774abd) had stacked a second stop ('Plan rubric passed... adapter=human') on top of the ledger, so a root task with the owner's approval on file still parked and three integration tests went red on main (test_gate_adjudicator_seat, test_owner_gate_click_records_design_approval); readiness speaks design-packet again and TaskDetailPage drops the dead root-approval banner."
    "\n7.13.123 (built as 7.13.118 by the daemon on task 8bcd4cb3, relabelled at rebase): model.ttl declares o:Bot, o:FSM, o:State, o:Transition, o:Behavior (Bot is NOT under o:Agent) with runsFsm/hasState/hasTransition/fromState/toState/partOf and an integer o:tier; model-lexicon.ttl carries the five Term rows, so the Structure and Vocabulary tabs show them and the aligner keeps Bot in a title (task 8bcd4cb3, epic 12029f92, owner 2026-08-27 mx-0e5a88)."
    "\n7.13.127: a promote_to_law run task's review gate (its ONE owner stop) showed BLOCKED - evidence not on file no matter how good the drafted rule was (owner 2026-08-27, live repro on task 44c7e2d0). Two bugs: (1) services/law_promotion.py's start_promotion() never set proof_type on the created task, so an unset proof_type fell into api/conductor.py:gate_readiness's generic trusted-runner EvidenceReceipt path, which a promote_to_law task can never satisfy -- fixed by declaring proof_type=\"review\" (a pure human sign-off, matching the workflow's own design). (2) gate_readiness's human-judgment branch then still demanded a green_gate-shaped screenshot/test-runner artifact for ANY demo/review-proof-type task regardless of which gate step it was actually at -- scoped that check to workflow_step==\"green_gate\" only, since promote_to_law's review step reviews a drafted TTL rule, not a code or UI change. New test test_review_gate_readiness_is_the_human_judgment_path_not_a_missing_receipt calls the real gate_readiness function, reproducing the live UI symptom, not just a stored field."
    "\n7.13.128: a task's own workflow (promote_to_law, triage, align_language, quickfix) never rendered its own FSM steps in the Implementation rail -- it always showed the \"implement\" workflow's 10-step SDLC (owner, live: \"where are all of the steps from the bots flow?\" / \"i thought promote to law bot has different fsm states, why would it look the same\"). Root cause: lib/useWorkflowDef.ts's useWorkflowSteps() took no argument and read only the top-level def.steps field off the cached GET /api/workflows response (the \"implement\"/conductor entry), regardless of which task's rail was rendering. Fixed by giving useWorkflowSteps(workflow?) a `workflow` argument that resolves steps from the SAME cached response's def.workflows[] catalog (each entry carries its own .steps), mirroring the backend's models.workflow.steps_for() alias join (models.task.WORKFLOW_ALIASES: \"implement\" -> the \"conductor\" catalog entry; every other named workflow already IS its own catalog id) -- falls back to the top-level steps for a blank/unknown value or an older service. Threaded through StepRail.tsx, SdlcProgress.tsx (+ its WorkflowsPage call site), PlanView.tsx's ConductorInfo/StepRail/SdlcProgress call sites, and TaskDetailPage.tsx's conductor object literal (task.workflow). ConductorPage.tsx's board tiles are left calling useWorkflowSteps() with no argument -- confirmed conductor_service.py's managed_tasks() (a POLICY_FILE) does not serialize a per-task workflow field, so that page cannot resolve one without a backend change out of this slice's scope; filed as a known gap. New test tests/unit/test_step_rail_uses_task_own_workflow.py source-reads all five files; retired test_workflows_section_ui.py::test_the_conductor_rail_consumes_the_hook's exact-no-args regex in place (superseded, not deleted) since the hook now legitimately takes an argument."
    "\n7.13.129: a plain Approve click (no override) on promote_to_law's review gate -- and triage's decide gate, same shape -- failed every time with \"gate has no validation kind\" (owner, live repro on task 44c7e2d0). review/decide are BY DESIGN plain owner gates with no rubric at all, so _verify_gate correctly returns verified=None (\"nothing to check\"), but gate_decide treated that None the same as an explicit verifier REJECTION and failed the gate. The error text said \"recover manually with override=True\", but the live Evidence tab has no override control at all, so the gate was permanently stuck for a human clicking the only Approve button the page has. Fix distinguishes the two None-returning cases in _verify_gate's result: validation=None (the step itself never declared a rubric -- by design) now passes on a plain approve; validation=<a real kind> with no wired _VERIFIER_RULES entry (a genuine configuration gap) is unchanged and still requires override=True. New test test_plain_approve_with_no_override_passes_the_review_gate, red-confirmed against the pre-fix code via a targeted Edit-based revert (never git stash/checkout). Full test_memory_promotes_to_law.py suite plus every one of the 40 test files referencing gate_decide (406 tests) green."
    "\n7.13.130: 7.13.129's fix only covered a FRESH pending plain-owner gate -- a gate already stuck gate_state=failed from that same bug (like the owner's own live task 44c7e2d0, hit during this session's own testing) goes through gate_decide's SEPARATE recovery branch, which needed the identical plain-owner-gate carve-out or it would refuse forever even after the first-approve bug was fixed -- exactly what this session's own \"Approve (recover)\" click reproduced live: same plain approve, same refusal, no override control anywhere on the page. New test test_a_review_gate_stuck_failed_from_the_old_bug_recovers_on_a_plain_approve, red-confirmed via a targeted Edit-based revert. Full 40-file gate_decide sweep (407 tests) green."
    "\n7.13.131: feat: a promoted SHACL rule's fixture proof becomes a durable, committed regression test, and the ontology records which test verifies which rule (owner: \"the ontology should work with the code and the rules to ensure that rules are covered by unit/int tests... tied back to the code, and that is where the ontology overlaps\"). services/law_promotion.py's _install_rule() already proved a drafted rule fires on a violating fixture and stays quiet on a compliant one before writing it into promoted-shapes.ttl; that proof only lived in memory. Now, once both checks pass, it also generates a SELF-CONTAINED pytest file (rule TTL + both fixtures embedded as string literals, not read from promoted-shapes.ttl at test time) under services/prism-service/tests/unit/law/ -- discovered by CI's own `pytest tests/unit` -- and appends `o:<rule> o:verifiedBy \"<path>::<test>\"` onto the rule's own URI in promoted-shapes.ttl. o:verifiedBy is declared alongside o:derivedFrom (the property every promoted rule/term already carries) in the same per-project promoted-model.ttl header -- that is where o:derivedFrom itself is actually declared (not model.ttl, despite it being the obvious first guess). ontology_rules.rule_catalog()/rule_decisions.decorated_report() surface a new verified_by field the same way they already surface derived_from, so a future UI pass is a pure rendering change. Extended test_installed_rule_fires_on_its_violating_fixture_and_stays_quiet_on_compliant to assert the generated file exists with the right shape, the o:verifiedBy triple lands in promoted-shapes.ttl, and rule_catalog() returns it; added test_generated_verification_test_for_the_promoted_rule_actually_runs_green, which shells out to a FRESH pytest subprocess against just the generated file and requires it to pass for real -- proof this produces a genuinely runnable regression test, not just well-formed-looking text."
    "\n7.13.132: a memory's human-facing name never became a clickable link anywhere it was mentioned -- only its raw mx-id did (owner: task 44c7e2d0's title read \"Promote ARC-PRISM-1 to law\", \"pet names like that can be used, but only if linked to reality\"). entity_linker.py's _build_index memory branch only ever registered a memory by its raw id; the Task branch two lines below already registers by BOTH id and label, and the module's own docstring already documented \"a task/memory TITLE matches\" as intended -- it was just never implemented for memory. Mirrors the existing Task pattern. New test_memory_name_links_the_same_as_its_raw_id, red-confirmed via a targeted Edit-based revert."
    "\n7.13.133: a rejected gate used to be a permanent dead end -- gate_decide(action=\"reject\") only set gate_state=\"failed\" and left workflow_step naming the gate, and task_runner.eligible_task() skips every gate step (pending, passed, OR failed) on purpose, so nothing ever redrove the task; the only recovery was a human hand-nudging workflow_step back (as happened live on task 12029f92's rejected plan_gate). Reject now rewinds workflow_step to the step immediately before the gate in the SAME workflow, clears gate_state to \"none\", and carries the rejection reason forward as gate_reason -- already spliced into every job dict by conductor_flow._job() regardless of step kind, so the redo pass reads why without a second fetch. Bounded by the existing green_gate MAX_AUTO_REWINDS budget (shared helper _consecutive_auto_rewinds), so a looping reject/redo pair cannot spin forever; also fixed that helper's own counting, which broke on the very first TaskService.update() audit row (action=\"updated\") interleaved in real history -- a pre-existing latent bug the green_gate auto-rewind path never hit in its own tests because their fake TaskService does not emit those rows. New test_gate_reject_rewinds_to_producing_step.py (3 tests, red-confirmed via targeted Edit-based revert); test_post_conductor_gate_reject_stores_reason updated in place for the new contract, with a note on what superseded it."
    "\n7.13.134: 7.13.133's rewind only covers a FRESH pending->reject -- a gate already stuck gate_state=\"failed\" from a standing reject STILL had no forward recovery when the underlying work genuinely needed a redo (task 12029f92 itself: rejected before 7.13.133 shipped, so its own fix never reaches it -- gate_decide unconditionally refused action=\"reject\" on an already-failed gate, override or not). The reject-and-rewind body is now a shared _reject_gate() method; gate_decide's failed-gate recovery branch calls it too when action=\"reject\" and override=True, reusing the identical producing-step rewind a fresh reject gets. override=True is an explicit human decision, not an unsupervised loop, so it bypasses MAX_AUTO_REWINDS (a plain reject, no override, on an already-failed gate still refuses, unchanged). New test_failed_gate_reject_override_recovers_via_rewind, red-confirmed via a targeted Edit-based revert."
    "\n7.13.135: 7.13.134's failed-gate reject+override recovery was backend-only -- TaskDetailPage.tsx's Reject button unconditionally unmounted the moment task.gate_state === \"failed\", so no real user could ever reach it (task 12029f92, the same rejected-before-the-fix ticket 7.13.134 targeted). Fixed by mirroring the neighboring Approve button's existing \"Approve (recover)\" shape: Reject now stays mounted on a failed gate when the gateOverride checkbox is ticked, stays disabled on a failed gate until override is ticked, and relabels to \"Reject (redo)\". New tests/unit/test_gate_reject_button_reaches_failed_gate_recovery.py source-reads the TSX and asserts the render guard, disabled expression, and label."
    "\n7.13.136: ship_worker's ci_wait treated a completely normal PENDING check as an immediate hard failure on its FIRST poll, never once entering its own retry-wait loop -- GH_CHECKS_PENDING_RC=8 is the documented gh CLI exit code for pending, but this machine's gh 2.4.0 (2022) returns rc=1 for the identical state, with stdout reading exactly \"checks\\tpending\\t0\\t<url>\" and empty stderr. Every real poll fell into the \"any OTHER ci_wait failure\" branch and failed at once, so the daemon's 3-consecutive-identical-failure circuit breaker blocked the task within a few sweeps while its CI was genuinely still running and later passed cleanly -- observed live on tasks dd2b87c8 and 1bc0b316, each needing a manual unblock mid-drive. Fixed by trusting the STATUS text over the exit code: a poll whose text contains a pending-like token (pending/queued/in_progress/waiting) now keeps polling regardless of rc, bounded by the same CI_TIMEOUT_S deadline as before; a genuine check failure (no pending token anywhere in the text) still fails immediately on its first poll, unchanged. New tests/unit/test_ship_worker_pending_status_text.py (2 tests, red-confirmed via a targeted Edit-based revert reproducing the exact live failure text) plus the full ship_worker sweep (35 tests) green."
    "\n7.13.137: a stale task.blocked_reason rendered a \"Blocked because\" banner on an actively working task -- observed live on 1bc0b316 seconds after 7.13.136 shipped: status=\"in_progress\", ship_worker genuinely rebasing/pushing/polling CI hands-off, while the header Card on TaskDetailPage.tsx still showed the OLD ship failure text because it rendered on `task.blocked_reason` alone, with no check on `task.status`. Owner: \"if work is happening in the system then the ticket should not say anything about blocked.\" The Evidence-tab card two screens down already had the right guard (`task.status === \"blocked\" && task.blocked_reason`); the header banner now matches it. New tests/unit/test_blocked_banner_honors_status_ui.py (2 source-reading tests, red-confirmed via a targeted Edit-based revert); real `npm run build` clean; neighboring TaskDetailPage suite (16 tests) green."
    "\n7.13.139: an epic-of-epics three or more levels deep read \"paused\"/\"stalled\" at every ancestor above the real work -- observed live on 95474ec7 (owner, watching the tab: \"something is wrong you have been idle on that task now for quite a long time, and it looks locked or frozen\"), whose actual driven leaf was 9b0f7c4b, three hops down (95474ec7 -> 3a3f90da -> 0e2c82f3 -> 9b0f7c4b). test_epic_activity_sees_its_driver.py (owner 2026-08-06) had already fixed the ONE-level case -- a direct child's own motion, or the epic's own linked session -- but ConductorService.activity_for's `active` check only read each direct child's OWN _task_motion_s, never that child's children, so a leaf's motion never propagated past its immediate parent. Every ancestor above that had no children of its own transitioning recently reported \"paused\"/\"stalled\", indistinguishable from a genuinely stuck task. Fixed with a depth-bounded (6) recursive _subtree_active(task) helper -- true if the task itself or ANY descendant at any depth is in_progress with a step transition inside the last 120s or a live drive heartbeat -- and the epic branch's `active` now calls it per direct child instead of reading motion directly. New tests/unit/test_epic_activity_sees_deep_descendants.py (3 tests: a 3-level-deep active leaf reads working at the root AND at every intermediate ancestor; a genuinely quiet deep epic still reads paused), red-confirmed against the un-recursed code. Full activity/heartbeat/epic-tagged sweep (36 tests) plus the 3-file gate-readiness/owner-actionable sweep (dirty-judge false-positive on first run, clean once committed) green."
    "\n7.13.140: an epic-rollup gate waiting on unfinished children read the alarm word BLOCKED, same moment as 7.13.139 above -- owner, live, immediately after that fix landed: \"no it reads blocked as best i can tell\", pointing at StepRail's green_gate pill reading BLOCKED while a great-grandchild was genuinely mid-implement_tasks. lib/gateSeverity.ts's shared four-label vocabulary (READY/PENDING/AWAITING YOU/BLOCKED) overloaded BLOCKED onto two different situations: a genuine stuck gate the owner must act on (verifier_refused, no evidence at all) and an epic-rollup refusal the owner can never act on directly (they cannot force a child to finish). Same reasoning as activity_for's \"idle\"/\"stalled\" wording fix (mx-dfd56a, owner 2026-07-21: \"render alarm vocabulary only when the owner actually has an action\"). Added a fifth label, WAITING ON CHILDREN (key waiting_on_children), scoped only to adapter===\"epic-rollup\" with a non-empty blocking_children list; every other blocked path (verifier_refused, generic no-evidence) is unchanged. StepRail's gate pill picks this up for free (it renders gateSeverity(...).label verbatim, no local hardcoding). TaskDetailPage.tsx's own special-cased epic-rollup headline literal (naming the blocking-child count) dropped its \"BLOCKED ·\" prefix to match. New tests/unit/test_epic_rollup_gate_severity_reads_waiting_not_blocked.py (3 tests, red-confirmed); test_gate_banner_refreshes_on_gate_arrival.py's _ALLOWED_LABELS/matrix updated in place (four labels -> five, epic_rollup_blocked renamed epic_rollup_waiting, noted as superseded). Full gate_severity/gate_banner/step_rail/task_detail/conductor_page/epic_rollup sweep (99 tests) plus a real `npm run build` green."
    "\n7.13.141: an epic page had no way to answer \"how much work is actually done\" without drilling through several rollup levels by hand -- owner, live, same session as 7.13.139/140: \"look at it as a user its impossobvle to see at this top level how much work is actualy done we have been waitinv fo almsot 2 hours\". The real live answer (3 of 7 descendants of 95474ec7 fully done, 2 more just reaching their own green_gate pending only a merge) existed but was invisible: `blocking_children` names only the ONE direct unfinished child, never the whole subtree. New `subtree_progress_counts(task_svc, root_id)` (conductor_service.py) recursively counts every live (non-cancelled/deleted) descendant's status at any depth, bounded (6, matching _subtree_active's own bound); `gate_readiness`'s epic-rollup branch (api/conductor.py) attaches it as `subtree_progress`, and TaskDetailPage.tsx renders a \"subtree progress\" line (\"N of M done · K in progress · J blocked\") above the existing blocked-on-children chip list. New tests/unit/test_epic_rollup_subtree_progress.py (5 tests: pure counting incl. cancelled-exclusion and a childless leaf, the readiness wiring, and a source-level render pin), red-confirmed; dirty-judge false-positive on first run against the readiness test, clean once committed. Full gate_severity/gate_banner/step_rail/task_detail/conductor_page/epic_rollup sweep (104 tests) plus a real `npm run build` green."
    "\n7.13.142: SdlcProgress.tsx's per-step clock said \"waiting 1:32:38\" directly beside a \"in progress\"/working state pill -- owner, live, same session as 7.13.139-141, after all three prior fixes: \"still hard to see it just looks like you have now been waiting for 90+ mins\". Root cause: `counting` (whether the animated execution clock runs) is gated on THIS task's own live token stream, but an epic-rollup can be genuinely state===\"working\" via _subtree_active's recursive descendant motion (7.13.139) while its own step streams zero tokens -- so `counting` stayed false and the not-counting branch's unconditional \"waiting\" label (a deliberate, tested wording from task 19c03bbc, chosen there specifically to avoid \"idle\"/\"stalled\") directly contradicted the state pill next to it. Fix is additive, not a reversal: the not-counting label now reads \"elapsed\" only when state===\"working\", every other not-counting state (the true parked case task 19c03bbc addressed) keeps \"waiting\" unchanged -- test_clock_renders_waiting_label_from_in_step_s_when_not_counting (that task's own regression pin) still passes verbatim. New tests appended to tests/unit/test_sdlc_clock_honest_when_parked.py (2 tests: the working-state branch says elapsed not waiting, red-confirmed via a targeted Edit-based revert; every other state keeps the original wording). Full sdlc/conductor_page/step_rail/task_detail/conductor_tile sweep (121 tests) plus a real `npm run build` green."
    "\n7.13.143 (task 9b0f7c4b, PR #2366): test_implement_preflight_has_identity_match went stale the moment this same task deliberately dropped prism_status from the `drive` MCP tool profile (prism_status is admin-only; a driving agent must never pay a ToolSearch load for it, the whole point of 9b0f7c4b) -- the test still hardcoded the literal string \"prism_status\" as a requirement, so a correct, deliberate change failed its own regression suite in CI (run 33135801669) and stranded the PR at green_gate for hours with no other real problem. The identity invariant the test protects -- the preflight must prove its MCP tools point at the SAME daemon as the conductor HTTP endpoint -- still holds: implement.js's preflight now reads the live PRISM_VERSION off prism_guide's own version banner (_version_banner(), prefixed onto every prism_guide() response, confirmed by direct source read) instead of prism_status's structured field, since prism_guide IS on the drive profile. Test updated in place to require \"prism_guide\" instead of \"prism_status\", with a comment naming what superseded it -- test_prototype_has_identity_guard is untouched (prototype.js was not part of this task's tool-profile change and still legitimately calls prism_status). Full test_prism_status_identity.py suite (6 tests) green."
    "\n7.13.144: tests/unit/test_implement_brain_first_retrieval_live.py's AC-2..AC-5 permanently red-gated EVERY PR's CI, unrelated to what that PR actually changed -- these tests can only be satisfied by a real fresh `implement` drive's transcripts (task 3a3f90da NFR-3, by design), which GitHub Actions CI structurally cannot provide, so a bare `pytest tests/unit` always hit the missing-env-var path. Confirmed blocking TWO unrelated PRs simultaneously: #2350 (task 85f92e4b, run 33044282088) and #2366 (task 9b0f7c4b, run 33135801669), same 4 tests, same reason, neither PR's own change at fault. NFR-3 chose a loud AssertionError specifically to forbid a VACUOUS PASS -- that intent survives intact: `_locate_drive_transcripts()` now calls `pytest.skip(...)` with the IDENTICAL diagnostic text only when PRISM_BRAIN_FIRST_DRIVE_SESSION is unset (never run, not a false pass); a deliberate opt-in that is then misconfigured (bad transcript root, or no transcripts found for a real session) still raises AssertionError, unchanged -- a skip is not a pass, it is pytest's own honest \"this check cannot run here\", which NFR-3 never forbade. New tests/unit/test_brain_first_retrieval_live_skips_without_session.py (3 tests: missing-session skips with the exact NFR-3 text preserved; the two misconfiguration paths still fail loudly), red-confirmed against the pre-fix source. Full test_implement_brain_first_retrieval_live.py + test_implement_brain_first_retrieval.py sweep green (4 skipped, not failed)."
