PRISM_VERSION_NOTES += (
    "\n7.13.148: review_previous_notes' premise_grounded rubric checked every claim for a citation but never checked the PREMISES against task.oracle at all (task 8956d6e4) -- owner, live, reading task 0b5dd37c's premises: \"i do not see any links in the premisi that go back to the orcal, its like you skilled that part entierly is that a missing step in theworkflow?\" Confirmed: score_green_outcome (the terminal green_gate) was the ONLY place task.oracle was ever checked against evidence, at the LAST step of a drive -- a citation-grounded premise set could sail through review_previous_notes, draft_story and verify_plan while never once engaging what the oracle actually asked, exactly what happened on 0b5dd37c (real file:line citations, zero mention of the oracle's own word \"tier\"). New score_oracle_engagement (arc_governance.py): splits task.oracle into clauses (numbered \"(1)(2)(3)\" markers, else sentence boundaries, else the whole oracle is one clause) and requires each clause to share oracle_min_shared_words meaningful words with the Premises section, OR be marked \"clause N: UNRESOLVED, <why>\" -- a named gap, not silence. Scoped narrowly: only fires when at least one claim carries a REAL evidentiary citation (file:line/PR/commit/output), never on a section whose claims are all REFUTED/UNVERIFIED/UNRESOLVED markers -- that scoping fixed a real regression this change first caused against ~20 pre-existing test fixtures that seed generic, admittedly-fake premise text unrelated to premise content (\"not a real premise claim - UNVERIFIED\"). _CLAIM_MARKER_RE now also accepts UNRESOLVED alongside REFUTED/UNVERIFIED, so the new clause-escape-hatch marker satisfies the existing citation-grounding tooth too, rather than colliding with it. New tests/unit/test_premise_grounded_oracle_engagement.py (17 tests: the worked example from 0b5dd37c's own shape, engaged/unresolved/blank-oracle/disabled paths, the marker-only skip, and real advance_task wiring). Full premise_grounded + arc_governance rubric sweep green."
)

PRISM_VERSION_NOTES += (
    "\n7.13.149: the Workflows directory sidebar rendered completely empty under real concurrent load (task 04359d5b) -- confirmed live via remote assist, /api/workflows itself answers in 60ms with real data when hit directly, so the backend was never the problem. A 300-entry browser network capture taken right after navigating to /workflows showed 13 near-simultaneous GET /api/conductor/state calls and zero /api/workflows calls -- the page's own one-time fetch never got a fair shot at the connection pool. Root cause: useConductorState's own MIN_REFRESH_MS throttle (task 4d399e0a) is per MOUNTED CONSUMER, not shared -- Sidebar.tsx (mounted on every page) and whichever page-level component also uses the hook (WorkflowsPage.tsx, ConductorPage.tsx) each run their own doLoad(), so one SSE push still fans out to N near-simultaneous identical requests. Added a module-scope in-flight-request map (_inFlight, useConductorState.ts) shared by every hook instance in the tab: a second concurrent caller for the same project now gets the SAME promise instead of starting its own fetch. Deliberately NOT a Context/store -- mx-d412e0 (task 40c29b83) already rejected hoisting this hook's state into one as heavier than the seam needs; this keeps that decision intact (grep -c \"conductor/state\" web_dist/assets/*.js is still exactly 1) while fixing the runtime duplication that decision's own success criterion (one call SITE in source) never actually measured. New tests/unit/test_conductor_state_request_coalescing.py (4 tests, red-confirmed against the pre-fix source via a temporary revert), full neighbouring useConductorState/Sidebar/LiveBar/ConductorPage suite (113 tests) green, real npm run build clean, grep-verified the coalescer survives minification's URL string intact."
)

PRISM_VERSION_NOTES += (
    "\n7.13.123: a fresh FAILED green receipt rewinds the drive to "
    "implement_tasks with the failing test ids; rewind_budget (default 3) "
    "parks it (task ad92c0e9)."
    "\n7.13.151: /workflows opened on a PARKED task instead of the work happening now. Measured live by the remote-assist player on 7.13.150: the board showed a WorkflowCore run from 32 hours earlier and the label LOADING RUN, while 31 tasks were in progress with 8 in implement_tasks and 9 in verify_plan, none of them visible. Cause: the auto-attach effect in WorkflowsPage.tsx matched a task whose gate_state was pending or failed. A task parked at a gate is waiting for a PERSON, not working, and openConductorInstance then sets viewingInstanceRef, which stops the definition poll from re-applying live occupancy to the whole canvas -- so one task awaiting review for 32 hours froze the entire board. The selector now matches only activity working or driving; with nothing working the board keeps live occupancy, which is the honest whole-board view. New tests/unit/test_workflows_opens_on_live_work.py (3 tests, red-confirmed against the pre-fix source). The neighbouring assertion in test_workflows_section_ui.py that required \"pending\" was retired in place with a note naming this task. 227 workflow tests green, real npm run build clean."
    "\n7.13.152: the Workflows board now SHOWS work moving, not just where it stands. WorkflowGraph.sendTransition has always been able to put a visible unit on an FSM edge and nothing ever called it from real work, so the canvas could render occupancy counts but never motion -- owner 2026-08-28: 'tokens and processing feeding the workflow ... Factorio'. WorkflowsPage now watches each managed task own workflow_step (already pushed over the conductor SSE) and sends exactly one unit down a transition when a task genuinely advances. One unit means one real advance, so a still board is honestly still, the same contract the ambient occupancy motion already keeps. First sighting of a task records only, a rewind with no forward token wire draws nothing, and the per-task map drops tasks that leave the board. Skipped while an instance overlay is open so a replay and board-wide traffic never mix on one screen. New tests/unit/test_workflows_belt_moves_on_real_advance.py (4 tests, red-confirmed: 3 failed before the change). 228 workflow tests green, tsc clean, real npm run build clean."
    "\n7.13.153: task_workspace.workspace_path did not exist, and its ABSENCE was a silent production defect. green_rewind._workspace_path falls back to it for any task row whose own workspace field is empty, which is most of them. getattr returned None, the helper returned an empty string, and oracle_spec.current_tree_sha of an empty path returns empty. Measured live across ten tasks parked at green_gate: every one reported an empty current tree while its checkout sat on disk under PRISM_DATA_DIR/task_workspaces. Two silent consequences. A PASSING receipt could never be confirmed fresh, so the gate refused it as unshipped and the task stalled indefinitely (8 of those 10 held a passing receipt). And maybe_rewind bailed at its if-not-tree guard, so the red-receipt rewind shipped as task ad92c0e9 never fired once in production despite being green, merged and deployed. Added a real resolver that consults the workspace index first (a checkout may live outside the default root) then the conventional path, returns empty when neither exists, and RESOLVES ONLY so a gate check can never materialise a workspace. New tests/unit/test_workspace_path_resolves_for_receipts.py (5 tests, all red before the change). 161 workspace, rewind, receipt, oracle and adjudicator tests green."
    "\n7.13.154: a stalled task whose work already shipped is now CLOSED, not split into children. Live waste on 2026-08-29: task 4cbac65a's fix landed on origin/main as b2f8d88f, out of band. The runner kept driving its verify_green_state step, stalled after three attempts, and split it into SIX children, one per pinned test, and every one of those tests was already green on main. Six pieces of work that could only ever be waste, sitting in a queue that drives one step per tick. _handle_stall now asks the question the gate already asks: is this task's own trailer reachable from origin/main? It delegates to the SAME squash-safe reader the unshipped tooth uses (api/tasks._shipped_sha_on_main), so the stall path and the gate can never disagree about whether a task landed. Shipped work is closed with a reason naming the commit. Unshipped work splits exactly as before, and the check FAILS CLOSED so a git or repo error leaves the old behaviour untouched rather than closing a task on an exception. New tests/unit/test_stall_split_skips_shipped_work.py (3 tests, 2 red before the change). 136 task-runner, stall and headless-drive tests green."
    "\n7.13.155: a task the shipped-ness tooth itself parked stays in the machine ship queue. ship_worker._eligible scanned only status in_progress, while the unshipped objection writes blocked_reason AND sets status blocked, so the gate could not pass until the branch landed and the branch could not land because the shipper never saw the task. Measured twice on 2026-08-29: eleven finished tasks stranded in the first pass, and seven again a few hours later, one of which was THIS task, whose own job is to fix the deadlock. Admission is now a POSITIVE match on that single bookkeeping objection (the invariant half of the message, no task id and no trailing advice, so a reworded tail cannot silently empty the queue), never on status blocked alone, which would put genuinely broken work back in the queue to fail again and starve healthy tasks. Tests: tests/unit/test_unshipped_block_stays_shippable.py, and 86 ship-suite tests green."
    "\n7.13.156: a rebase conflict whose only casualty is __version__.py now resolves itself. Every task bumps PRISM_VERSION and appends a changelog entry at the same place, so any two tasks in flight collide there. Measured 2026-08-29: three tasks blocked at the ship rebase and the version file was a casualty in two of them. This SUPERSEDES the refusal recorded in _rebase_onto_main from task 229954e4, which declined to touch any conflict, not even two branches bumping the same version, on the grounds that a silent guess is worse than parking. That reasoning holds for real content and does not hold here: the merge is mechanical, not a judgement. The resolver takes the origin/main file, which already carries every entry that shipped, bumps its patch by exactly one, and appends the branch's own new entries. Nothing is invented, nothing is discarded, and two tasks can never claim one version. It refuses unless the conflict set is EXACTLY the version file, refuses if the merged module does not parse, and any other conflict parks exactly as before. New tests/unit/test_version_only_rebase_conflict_resolves.py (3 tests, 2 red before the change) asserting the resolver ran rather than a rebase merely succeeding. 86 ship-suite tests green."
    "\n7.13.157: CORRECTS 7.13.156. The version-only rebase resolver read the merge stages backwards. During a rebase git replays each commit ONTO the upstream, so stage 2 is origin/main and stage 3 is the task's own commit. The first version had them swapped, so it took the BRANCH's version literal as the base and dropped every changelog entry main had gained since the branch was cut. Measured: it rewrote two task branches to PRISM_VERSION 7.13.125 while main was at 7.13.156, deleting 115 changelog lines. Neither shipped, and only because both were blocked for other reasons. The original test passed because its fixture gave BOTH sides the same version literal, so reading either stage produced the same number and the assertion could not tell them apart. The fixture now gives main 7.13.140 and the branch 7.13.101 and expects 7.13.141, which fails loudly on the old code. It also asserts the shared history survives and that no entries are dropped. The two damaged branches were reset and re-rebased on the corrected resolver. 86 ship-suite tests green."
    "\n7.13.158: the version-conflict resolver now resolves EVERY conflict in a replay, not just the first. A branch with several commits touching __version__.py hits a fresh conflict on each replayed commit. Observed live on task afb47c33: the first conflict resolved, rebase --continue walked 3/5, 4/5, 5/5 and stopped on another version conflict, the single-pass resolver returned False, and the caller aborted and parked the task. Resolution now loops while the conflict set stays EXACTLY the version file, bounded at 20 passes as a safety net rather than an expected limit, and hands back False the moment any other path appears. ONE BRANCH CLAIMS ONE VERSION: only the first pass bumps, so a five-commit branch advances the number by one rather than by five, and the version keeps saying something about release order instead of about a branch's shape. New test builds a branch with two version-touching commits and asserts the rebase FINISHES, leaving no rebase-merge state behind. 86 ship-suite tests green."
    "\n7.13.159: the version-conflict resolver completes its rebase step on git 2.34 AND on git 2.43. The two resolver tests passed on this dev box, whose git is 2.34, and failed on ubuntu-24.04 CI, whose git is newer, for four consecutive runs -- and because those tests live on main, every open pull request went red with them. The two git versions disagree about which editor knob a rebase --continue consults: the older apply backend honours core.editor, the newer merge backend reaches for the commit-message editor. The step now sets BOTH core.editor and sequence.editor, and if the continue still refuses it commits the staged resolution with --no-edit and continues. The tests now print git status and git --version in their failure message, so a CI-only failure reports its own environment instead of costing another round trip. Same local-versus-CI git split already recorded against task 3f46342a."
    "\n7.13.160: a killed step says it was killed, and a step that RUNS the suite gets room to finish. Epic 9f60a849, whose own title is that drives finish without a human nudge, stalled three times at verify_green_state needing exactly that nudge. The recorded outcome each time was exit=-9, which is SIGKILL: the step hit the 900 second wall clock while running the whole suite. The host had 85 GB free and no OOM kills, so it was the clock and nothing else. The task then reported 'no red test id was named in the last proof' -- the fallback text for a stall with nothing to split on -- which sends whoever reads it hunting for a test problem that does not exist. A stall whose last recorded outcome is exit=-9 now says the step was KILLED, names the budget it exceeded, and names the variable that raises it. The message is read from durable history, so a restart between the kill and the stall still tells the truth, and any error keeps the old text because a wrong 'killed' claim would be its own dishonesty. verify_green_state now gets 3x the operator's chosen budget and implement_tasks 2x, since both RUN things rather than write about them -- the implement workflow already knew this and the task runner did not. The operator's variable still governs the base. 4 new tests in test_slow_step_budget_and_honest_stall.py, 3 red before the change, and the fourth pins that an ordinary stall keeps its old wording."
    "\n7.13.161: the version-conflict rebase resolver now completes on git 2.55 too. CI (git 2.55.0) ignored the -c core.editor form that satisfied 2.34 and 2.43, so rebase --continue never completed and both portability tests in test_version_only_rebase_conflict_resolves.py went red on main -- which then blocked task 72ccaf94 at ci_wait, since a red main is a red branch. GIT_EDITOR and GIT_SEQUENCE_EDITOR are now set in the ENVIRONMENT (via env, so _run keeps its (runner, argv, cwd) shape); the environment knob outranks the config one and every git version honours it. The failure could not be reproduced on this box (git 2.34), so the resolver also records git OWN words in _LAST_RESOLVE_ERROR and the park message appends them -- a CI-only failure now names itself instead of being guessed at a third time. "
    "\n7.13.162: the implement_tasks stall splitter may only create children for THIS task own tests, and may not create the same child twice. Live on task 72ccaf94: its completion_proof was a verify report that listed ten pytest node ids from suites the slice never touched (test_auto_updater, test_lifespan_lock_recovery, test_pidfile_lifecycle, test_task_page_bundle) because the step had noted which unrelated suites were already failing. _handle_stall read that task-level field, made a child per id, and made them AGAIN on the next stall -- 30 children in three identical sets of ten, none able to move the parent, while the owner watched the board grow and said this is what will cause the system to crash. Now the ids are intersected with the task own verify list (a task that pins nothing keeps the old split-everything contract, which test_task_runner_stall_detection still covers), and ids already covered by an OPEN child are skipped. New test_stall_split_only_splits_this_tasks_own_tests.py: 3 tests, red at e19ddedc, green here. 83 stall/runner tests stay green. "
    "\n7.13.164: plan_gate now asks three DETERMINISTIC questions before a machine seat approves, each one a defect a human caught by hand on task 72ccaf94 across five plan rounds. absent_file_claim refuses a plan that says a path does not exist when that path resolves (round 1 said tests/unit/test_sqlite_maint.py did not exist; it sat at line 34). stop_if_pinned refuses a stop_if test that task.verify does not pin, so the slice own named risk is held by its own gate. already_green_ac refuses an acceptance criterion offered as the observation for an oracle clause when it ALREADY PASSES at the base commit -- rounds 2, 3 and 4, and again at green_gate, where AC-7 passed at the tests-only commit while claiming to observe the soak clause. The checks live in services/plan_gate_checks.py, which is NOT gate policy by construction: it returns refusal STRINGS and never decides a gate. .prism/behaviors/conductor/plan-gate-check.json goes to version 2 and renders one node per check on the Workflows page, each served by the new /api/workflows/steps/plan-gate-check-one endpoint -- the same contract /steps/green-gate-check already uses, always HTTP 200 so a refused tooth is a REPORTED fact and the chain reaches every later check. Both machine seats (api/conductor_flow entry-time autoclear, gate_adjudicator re-sweep) read the same verdict, so they agree about why a plan did not clear. DEGRADE RULE: a checker that cannot measure reports PASS with a recorded reason -- a plan is never refused because the tooling failed. PRISM_PLAN_GATE_MEASURE_BASE=0 leaves the cheap declaration tier only. A human Approve goes through gate_decide and is never blocked here. "
    "\n7.13.165: brain.db stopped growing without bound. Measured 2026-08-28: the projects directory held 18 GB for about 50 MB of real content, one brain.db was 2.9 GB for 1,419 documents and 3.5 MB of text, with 306,959 FTS segment rows, a 1.7 GB freelist and a 750 MB -wal that never truncated. Four causes, each fixed at its own layer. SQLite defaults PRAGMA recursive_triggers OFF, so the INSERT OR REPLACE in Brain._ingest_single deleted the old docs row WITHOUT firing docs_fts_ad and every changed document left its superseded index entry behind at a rowid no docs row occupies; sqlite_db.connect now sets that pragma, and journal_size_limit (PRISM_SQLITE_JOURNAL_SIZE_LIMIT, default 64 MB) so a WAL a reader keeps pinned can hand its space back on a PASSIVE checkpoint. BrainService.index_doc now keeps a per-file content hash and returns skipped plus the doc id without writing when the content is byte-identical, instead of deleting and re-inserting every chunk on every drift pass. checkpoint_db runs a bounded docs_fts merge BEFORE the checkpoint, never after, for the same reason PRAGMA optimize already ran there: it writes, and a write after the TRUNCATE undoes the fold. And checkpoint_db no longer reports success when the pragma comes back busy=1, which is how a store grew a 750 MB -wal while every maintenance pass logged a clean run. Stores already carrying orphans self-heal once on open, marked in index_meta so it never repeats, via delete-all plus a re-insert through expand_identifiers -- NOT the rebuild form, which reads the raw content column and destroys every split identifier token (measured: hits for 'Gamma' fall from 2 to 0). New tests/unit/test_brain_fts_no_orphans_on_reindex.py (10 tests, red before the change), 101 neighbouring brain, sqlite and maintenance tests green."
    "\n7.13.166: plan_gate stop_if_pinned no longer refuses a slice that IS correctly pinned. Found live by exercising the tooth on 7.13.165 against task 72ccaf94 itself: its stop_if names the test FUNCTION test_checkpoint_db_truncates_wal while task.verify pins the FILE that defines it, tests/unit/test_sqlite_maint.py, and the check compared a function name against a file stem -- so the tooth would have blocked the very plan whose defects it was built from. A test_ token is now resolved to the pinned file that DEFINES it. Resolution may only ADD a way to pass: with no root, or a verify naming no .py file, the pinned-file list is empty and the textual verdict stands, so a function defined nowhere is still refused. "
    "\n7.13.167: task_history stopped growing without bound, and 290 MB came back. Measured on prism/tasks.db: 155,659 rows for 841 tasks and 110 MB, growing about 19,000 rows and 10 MB per DAY, with 86 percent of it exact duplicates. The driver is the 60 s gate-adjudicator sweep. For each task parked at green_gate it re-attempts the approve, is refused for an unchanged reason, and wrote a gate_decide row PLUS an updated row every sweep forever. Ten parked tasks made about 12 rows a minute. _record_history now COLLAPSES an identical consecutive repeat, advancing the existing row timestamp instead of adding a row. The timestamp still moves because task motion is read from these rows, so suppressing them outright would make live work read as dead. A changed detail, a changed actor and a changed action all still append. RECOVERY, same pass, run with the daemon stopped and tasks.db backed up first: 133,909 duplicate rows pruned keeping the FIRST and LAST of every identical run, then an FTS5 optimize (segment merge only, never rebuild, which re-reads raw content and destroys expand_identifiers), then VACUUM, then wal_checkpoint TRUNCATE in that order because an optimize writes. 804 MB to 513 MB across every project: prism/tasks.db 146 to 40, prism/graph.db 47 to 11, prism/brain.db 136 to 106. Verified after: integrity_check ok on every file, orphan scan rc=0, 841 tasks intact, and identifier expansion survived (Brain 1699 hits AND BrainEngine 27, the split a rebuild would have destroyed). "
    "\n7.13.168: 7.13.167 did not stop the task_history growth and the measurement said so: 25.6 rows/min after, against about 42 before. Two corrections. FIRST, the repeat is not CONSECUTIVE. Each sweep writes a three-row cycle per parked task and the gate_reason values alternate, updated(STALE receipt), gate_decide(refused), updated(trailer not reachable), so no two adjacent rows ever match and the adjacent-pair collapse never fired. _record_history now looks back over a window of 8 rows and MOVES the duplicate to the end (delete plus append) rather than updating it in place, because consumers read this table ORDER BY id and expect the timestamps to ascend with it. SECOND, and the reason to be careful here: advance_task and gate_decide are NOT audit rows. The conductor reconstructs workflow and gate state by reading them back, and collapsing them turned test_gate_decide_refuses_when_state_not_pending from failed into task is not currently on a gate step. Collapsing is now restricted to updated and ste_normalise, which carry 69 MB of the 83 MB and which nothing reads as state. A deliberate trade, pinned by a test: an oscillating state keeps ONE row per distinct value, so the history says what the state is and what it was before, not how many times it flipped. 131 green across every suite that reads task_history. "
    "\n7.13.169: the gate sweep no longer re-attempts a refusal that cannot change. This is the SOURCE of the task_history growth that 7.13.167 and 7.13.168 only made cheaper to store. Every interval the sweep re-attempted every pending gate and, on refusal, wrote a gate_decide row plus an updated row. Some of those gates the machine seat can NEVER decide: a demo or review proof_type is human-only by owner rule eaafdf75 and adjudicate_green_gate returns None for it BY DESIGN, so three parked tasks were each re-attempted 1,440 times a day for a verdict that cannot exist. The delay now doubles per unchanged pass from 60 s to a 30 min cap. Anything that ACTUALLY changes about the task, a freshly minted receipt, a push, a human edit, moves its updated_at and resets the backoff to the next sweep, so this delays repetition and never a real decision. In-memory and per-process: a restart re-attempts everything once, which is the correct bias. 104 green across the gate, adjudicator and task_history suites. "
    "\n7.13.171: minting evidence no longer dirties the workspace it is judged in. Task 84a91b0b. Reproduced deterministically on task f61617c1: clear the worktree (git status --porcelain 0), POST /api/conductor/gate/mint, read it again (1), and readiness answers that an uncommitted change remains in the task own workspace so it cannot be shipped. Running the pinned suite by hand did NOT dirty the tree; only the mint did. CAUSE: law_promotion._write_verification_test writes to task_workspace._prism_repo_root(), which ascends from ITS OWN MODULE, so an oracle running inside a task worktree imports that worktree copy of prism_service and the root resolves to the WORKTREE. The generated law test landed in the very tree the green_gate cleanliness tooth was about to judge. Two tasks that were green, cleanly rebased and already merged to origin/main could not close on it. Now the writer refuses any root that is a task workspace, and compares before writing so an unchanged generated test is not rewritten at all. A real checkout still gets the file, and a CHANGED rule still writes. 113 green across the law suites. "
    "\n7.13.172: 7.13.171 did not fire and the repro said so. Clearing the worktree and minting still left it dirty, because that guard compared the resolved root against task_workspace._root() and resolve_data_dir() answers differently inside the oracle own subprocess, so the workspaces path it computed never matched the real one. The guard now keys on a filesystem fact no config can disagree with: a LINKED git worktree has .git as a FILE, a primary checkout has it as a DIRECTORY. The explicit workspaces check stays as a second line. Also measured while confirming this: the derivedFrom memory id is a NEW value on every pass (main holds mx-06132c, two successive runs wrote mx-9f1fd6 then mx-fa33a3), so the generated bytes differ each time and no compare-before-write alone could have stopped the churn. 115 green. "
    "\n7.13.173: the remote-assist bridge is drivable over REST, not only over one MCP tool profile. A session connected on the interactive profile could LIST a live bridge session through GET /api/agent-bridge/sessions and had no way whatever to drive it, because agent_bridge_command sits in a different profile and no route existed to fall back to. POST /api/agent-bridge/sessions/{id}/commands is the REST twin of that tool and authenticates the SAME way, on the caller real principal through session_owned_by, the existing holding-the-key model. It deliberately does NOT take the session own bearer token the way /results and the SSE route do: those are the BROWSER talking back, where no header can be attached, and GET /sessions never hands the token out. For that reason the path is also excluded from _is_agent_bridge_session_path, so it stays INSIDE the general auth gate rather than being waved through it. The action set moved onto the service as KNOWN_ACTIONS so the two callers cannot drift about what is drivable. 91 green across the agent-bridge suites. "
    "\n7.13.174: a parked step no longer paints progress it is not making. Owner watching a task sit at story_gate: the progress animation just fills and fills and fills but never actually finishes, and it shows work being done when no work is being done. MEASURED on task 54585a5f parked at story_gate over 75 wall seconds: in_step_s ran 245.5 to 321.9 while PARKED and the segment filled 0.900 to 0.951, climbing toward the 0.97 asymptote it can never pass. task_motion_s tracked in_step_s almost exactly, so frozenInStep was about zero and the execution clock beside the bar correctly printed no execution time at all: one step, one second, two numbers contradicting each other. The parked branch froze the CLOCK (setLiveInStep(frozenInStep)) but computed the FILL from the raw server in_step_s, which keeps growing while the ball is in someone else own court. liveFraction now takes a seconds override and the parked branch feeds it frozenInStep, so the bar advances only for time the task was really executing. Same fault the clocks themselves had already had fixed once. Pinned against the TSX source (this SPA has no JS test runner) by parsing the parked branch by brace depth, never a fixed window, so a comment cannot satisfy the assertion. 371 green across the suites reading SdlcProgress/phase_progress; tsc and the real npm build both rc=0. "
    "\n7.13.175: the flow node progress bar is no longer an animation loop. Owner, watching a task parked at plan_gate: the progress has filled to full like 15 times, it is not a progress it is just an animation loop, it should never fill up and then cycle to start again unless the sub tasks in that flow step are actually getting done. THE MECHANISM was WorkflowsPage 0.12 + ((elapsedSeconds % 18) / 18) * 0.68 for any step with no duration history: a pure wall-clock sawtooth sweeping 12 to 80 percent and snapping back every 18 SECONDS, forever, whatever the step was doing. That is about 15 cycles in the 4.5 minutes the owner watched, and it is the same wiggle behind the earlier Build and test stuck cycling at 23 percent after 4m51s report. With real pacing the bar still maths to full ONCE and stops at 0.98; without it there is no honest number, so it draws none and the card RUN clock carries the running signal. drawNode also paints no body fill and no leading-edge marker for an indeterminate step, because a painted width IS a claim about how far along the step is. Also fixed the p95 test that broke on this: it sliced a FIXED 2000-char window that had already been widened once for the same reason, and now parses the block by brace depth. 177 green; tsc and the real npm build both rc=0. "
    "\n7.13.176: a gate is now a state that is WORKED, by a real seat, in named flow states. Owner: the gate itself is its own state the conductor bot can hand a task to, and the gate should be working a -p claude instance that is the adjudicator; on the layering, it should be inferred AND rubric, make every state the MOST deterministic it can be by codifying as much as it can and leave room for inference to deal with unknowns; and on the shape, when you codify you had better be making new agentic states in the flow, because bot to (agentic flow state or bot) is progressive and infinitely hierarchical. Before this, task_runner REFUSED gates outright (NEVER decides a gate; if step type is gate, skip), so a gate had no worker at all, which is also why its progress had nothing real to show. Now .prism/behaviors/conductor/gate-adjudication.json gives the conductor bot two visible states: codified, everything the rubric can decide, and infer, the residue a rubric cannot express, judged by a read-only claude -p seat. ORDERING IS THE POINT: infer is reached ONLY when codified had nothing to say. A codified refusal is already a decision and inference may not talk it away. The seat is READ-ONLY (Read/Glob/Grep, never Write/Edit/Bash) because a seat that edits the tree it judges is the 7.13.171 bug; it reports as its own actor so no-self-review still holds; unparseable output is NO decision, never an approval; and a demo or review green_gate stays human-only per owner rule eaafdf75. Off unless PRISM_GATE_AGENT_ENABLED=1. 529 green across the gate, workflow and behaviour suites. "
    "\n7.13.177: the Workflows rail is clickable by an agent, and the adjudicator behaviour actually renders. Two gaps found by trying to SHOW the owner the new bot. FIRST, a behaviour FILE is invisible on its own: the catalog enumerates fsm.behaviorIds out of .prism/behaviors/conductor/bot.json, so gate-adjudication existed, was in _CONDUCTOR_LINKED_BEHAVIOR_IDS, and GET /api/workflows still listed eleven conductor children without it. It is now declared on the pipeline FSM, and a test refuses ANY orphaned behaviour file. SECOND, the rail buttons carried no id, no data attribute and no aria-label, while the remote-assist bridge resolves selectors with a plain document.querySelector (CSS only, no text matching), so an agent could SEE an entry in a screenshot and had no way to click it -- owner: CLICK ON IT AS A USER WOULD, that is why you have remote assist. Both rail levels now carry data-workflow-id. 138 green; tsc and the real npm build both rc=0. "
    "\n7.13.178: adjudication moved INSIDE the gates, and the hierarchy stopped being two levels. Owner, pointing at the rail: that is not part of a gate step, that folder like the steps is hierarchical, and should not be at that level since that is not a connected step in the bot top level behavior flow. Correct, and the rule already existed: changelog 7.12.16 says progressive disclosure under conductor should only show what an actual state CALLS via linked_workflow_id, and that nesting something no state calls claims a link that does not exist. gate-adjudication as a top-level peer is deleted. Every gate state already links to its own behaviour, and those behaviours steps ARE that gate codified layer, so infer is now the LAST state inside each of story-gate-check, plan-gate-check, red-gate-status and green-gate-status, after everything that gate could codify. SECOND, and the bigger one: owner said you seem to think there are only two layers when they are infinitely nested as needed to resolve our work. The navigation was never the cap (workflowPath is an appended array with per-level breadcrumbs); the DATA was. Only the conductor own ten states carried linked_workflow_id, from a hardcoded chain, so every behaviour step was a leaf and the tree could never exceed conductor to behaviour to steps. A behaviour step now declares its own linkedWorkflowId, so depth is bounded by the work and not by the renderer. 924 green. "
    "\n7.13.179: every layer of the flow can now answer what it has actually RUN. Owner: the rail should render the execution instances of the bot layer we are looking at, and each time we click into the next layer it should move to that historical view for that instance. The blocker was real and the page said so in its own comment: /runs/history 404s for anything but validation, no WorkflowCore run ever backs a declarative FSM behaviour, so every bot-family entry showed an empty rail forever. But the executions DID happen and ARE recorded. Measured on this project: 2,012 gate_decide and 3,246 advance_task rows. GET /api/workflows/{id}/instances answers them per layer, a gate instances being its own gate_decide rows and a step instances being the advance_task rows that LEFT it, scoped by task_id so drilling in from one task shows THAT task history at the deeper layer rather than every task. The behaviour-to-step map is the linked_workflow_id chain inverted, pinned by a test against that same chain so the two cannot drift. This is the DATA half; the rail still renders the catalog, and wiring it to this endpoint is the next slice. 144 green. "
)
